INT. DUGOUT CHAPEL - WESTERN FRONT - NIGHT

A cellar dug under a ruined church. Sandbags for pews. A cracked crucifix propped on an ammunition crate. A gramophone, hand-cranked, plays one worn waltz. Candle stubs. The earth trembles with a distant shelling.

CHAPLAIN ROSARIO VELAZQUEZ, 40s, kneels at the crate, adjusting the crucifix. His hands are quick and wrong.

PRIVATE EDMUND "TEDDY" LACROIX, 19, sits on a sandbag with a letter on his knee and a pencil behind his ear.

SERGEANT NIKOLAI BORISOV, 30s, leans on the entrance, rifle slung, helmet dented.

TEDDY
Father. How do you write to someone you haven't spoken to in a year?

CHAPLAIN VELAZQUEZ
You begin with the weather.

TEDDY
It's raining.

CHAPLAIN VELAZQUEZ
Then that's a start.

TEDDY
She won't want to know about the rain.

CHAPLAIN VELAZQUEZ
Nobody wants to know about the rain. That's why it is safe.

BORISOV
Safe is a good word for it.

CHAPLAIN VELAZQUEZ
Sergeant. You may sit.

BORISOV
I'll stand.

TEDDY
What if I tell her the truth?

CHAPLAIN VELAZQUEZ
Which truth?

TEDDY
That I'm afraid.

BORISOV
She knows that.

TEDDY
How?

BORISOV
Everyone's afraid. The ones who say they're not are the ones lying.

CHAPLAIN VELAZQUEZ
Sergeant Borisov is a wise man.

BORISOV
Sergeant Borisov is a tired man.

TEDDY
Are you afraid, Sergeant?

BORISOV
I don't discuss it.

TEDDY
That's an answer.

BORISOV
Take it however you like.

CHAPLAIN VELAZQUEZ
Edmund. Write what you would say if she were standing here.

TEDDY
She would never be standing here.

CHAPLAIN VELAZQUEZ
Then write that.

The shelling swells. Dust sifts from the beams. The gramophone skips, and starts again.

TEDDY
Dear Mathilde. It is raining.

CHAPLAIN VELAZQUEZ
Good.

TEDDY
There is a waltz playing. It's the only one we have.

BORISOV
It's a bad waltz.

TEDDY
It's the only one.

CHAPLAIN VELAZQUEZ
Keep going.

TEDDY
I think of you at the window. I think of you hanging the washing. I think of you not thinking of me, and I'm glad.

BORISOV
Glad?

TEDDY
That she's not afraid for me.

A very long silence. Then Borisov crosses the cellar. Sits beside Teddy on the sandbag. Takes the pencil from behind the boy's ear, hands it to him, and says nothing more.
